<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsushima Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsushima is a mountainous island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> Strait between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and the Korean peninsula, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> Prefecture. Its rulers, the Sō clan, depended on trade with Korea and managed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s relations with the Joseon court, including the trading post at Busan. The island was raided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1274 and attacked by Korea in 1419, and the Japanese navy destroyed the Russian fleet in the strait nearby in 1905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsushima-island.jpg" alt="Tsushima Island" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image Science and Analysis Laboratory, NASA-Johnson Space Center. &quot;The Gateway to Astronaut Photography of Earth. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsushima_Island_ISS034.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tsushima lies between the Tsushima Strait and the Korea Strait, roughly halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and the Korean Peninsula. The Korea Strait splits at the archipelago into two channels, and the wider one, closer to the Japanese mainland, is the Tsushima Strait. The main island, once a single island, was divided in two in 1671 by the Ōfunakoshiseto canal and in three in 1900 by the Manzekiseto canal, forming North Tsushima and South Tsushima. The group also includes over 100 smaller islands.</p><p>It measures about seventy by fifteen kilometres. Forest covers about 90 per cent of the island, and there are few plains, so the islanders have lived mainly by fishing and trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Japanese mythology Tsushima was one of the eight original islands created by the deities Izanagi and Izanami. Archaeological evidence suggests that settlers from the Japanese archipelago and the Korean Peninsula lived there from the Jōmon period to the Kofun period. The Chinese Records of the Three Kingdoms describes a country called Duì hǎi guó, commonly identified with Tsushima, as one of about 30 that made up the Yamatai union. Its families controlled Iki Island and traded with Yayoi Japan. A mountain castle on Jouyama, built in 667 by Emperor Tenji, housed border guards, and from the early sixth century Tsushima was an official province of Japan.</p><p>In the feudal period it was an important trading centre. Private trade between Goryeo, Tsushima, Iki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a> halted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasions-of-japan">Mongol invasions of Japan</a> between 1274 and 1281, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goryeosa">Goryeosa</a> records that in 1274 Korean troops of the Mongol army led by Kim Bang-gyeong killed a great number of people on the islands. Tsushima became one of the main bases of the Wokou, Japanese pirates, along with Iki and Matsuura. Korea at times placated the pirates with trade agreements and at times used force, and in 1389 General Pak Wi of Goryeo wiped out the Wokou on Tsushima.</p><p>In 1861 the Imperial Russian Navy tried to establish a base on the island, in what is known as the Tsushima incident. The plan had been made by Vice Admiral Ivan Likhachev, but the Russians withdrew under pressure from Vice Admiral John Hope, who commanded the British ships in Japanese waters on the orders of Sir Rutherford Alcock.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tsushima is part of Nagasaki Prefecture, and Tsushima City, formed on 1 March 2004 from six towns, covers the whole island. Izuhara was the castle town of the Tsushima domain until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, and ancient stone walls still stand in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> street. The Ofunaeato ruins, docks created in 1663, served the Korean trade and the alternate attendance system. The sacred Mount Shiratake, 519 metres high, is a national natural monument for its primeval forest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tsushima Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It traded with Joseon Korea, using the daimyō of <b>Tsushima Island</b> as middlemen, and with imperial China, where goods were exchanged and labelled as offerings from one emperor to another.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign trade was tightly limited to tribute missions to Beijing and the Japanese trading post at Busan, run by the Sō clan of <b>Tsushima</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Further contacts with Korea, the Ryukyu Kingdom and the Ainu were channelled through the domains of <b>Tsushima</b>, Satsuma and Matsumae.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The So family had ruled <b>Tsushima</b>, between Kyushu and Korea, since the 13th century and depended on trade with Korea.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsushima-fuchu-domain">Tsushima-Fuchu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
